{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"structured-bindings","title":"Structured Bindings","group":"Language Basics","order":11,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">C++17 <code>auto [a, b] = expr;</code> unpacks a pair, tuple, array or plain struct into named variables in one line.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-package\"></i>What can be unpacked</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;map&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;tuple&gt;\n\nstruct Point { int x; int y; };\n\nstd::tuple&lt;int, double, std::string&gt; record() { return {7, 9.5, \"ok\"}; }\n\nint main() {\n    auto [x, y] = Point{3, 4};                  // aggregate struct: by member order\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; x + y &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    int arr[3] = {10, 20, 30};\n    auto [a, b, c] = arr;                        // C array\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; a &lt;&lt; b &lt;&lt; c &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    auto [id, score, status] = record();         // tuple\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; id &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; score &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; status &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    std::map&lt;std::string, int&gt; ages{{\"ada\", 36}, {\"bob\", 25}};\n    for (const auto&amp; [name, age] : ages)         // pairs in a map\n        std::cout &lt;&lt; name &lt;&lt; '=' &lt;&lt; age &lt;&lt; ' ';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    if (auto [it, inserted] = ages.insert({\"ada\", 99}); !inserted)\n        std::cout &lt;&lt; \"ada already \" &lt;&lt; it-&gt;second &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>7\n102030\n7 9.5 ok\nada=36 bob=25\nada already 36</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-link\"></i>Copy or reference?</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;utility&gt;\nint main() {\n    std::pair&lt;int, int&gt; p{1, 2};\n    auto [a, b] = p;      // binds to a hidden *copy* of p\n    a = 100;\n    auto&amp; [c, d] = p;     // binds to p itself\n    c = 50;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; p.first &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; p.second &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>50 2</code></pre></figure>\n<ul><li><code>auto [..]</code> — copy of the whole object; <code>auto&amp; [..]</code> — refer to the original; <code>const auto&amp; [..]</code> — read-only view.</li><li>The number of names must match exactly; you can't skip one (use a dummy name like <code>_</code> or <code>[[maybe_unused]]</code>).</li><li>For your own classes with private members, enable it by specialising <code>std::tuple_size</code>/<code>std::tuple_element</code> and providing <code>get&lt;N&gt;</code> (advanced).</li></ul>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How would you swap-return two values from a function in modern C++?</summary><div><p>Return a <code>std::pair</code>/<code>std::tuple</code> or, better, a small named <code>struct</code>, and unpack at the call site with structured bindings: <code>auto [quotient, remainder] = divide(17, 5);</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}